1. hosts decides where the tasks should run.

For example:

- hosts: all

or:

- hosts: app_key

or:

- hosts: webservers

2. become: true

You have:

become: true

This tells Ansible:

"Use elevated privileges when executing the tasks."

In simple Linux terms, think of it as:

sudo

For example, installing Apache requires administrator/root privileges.

3. name

name: Install Apache

This is just a description of the task.

It makes the playbook easier for humans to understand.

4. Ansible Module

This part:

ansible.builtin.apt:

is the module Ansible uses for the task.

Ansible provides many modules for different jobs.

For example:

apt       → package management
copy      → copy files
file      → manage files/directories
service   → manage services
user      → manage users
template  → deploy templates

5. Your task is:

- name: Install Apache
  ansible.builtin.apt:
    name: apache2
    state: present
    update_cache: yes

=> Let's break it down.

a) name: apache2

This tells the apt module which package you want.

In this case:

Package = apache2

b) state: present

It means:

"Make sure Apache is installed."

It does not simply mean "run the install command every time."

If Apache is already installed, Ansible understands that the desired state has already been achieved.

If Apache isn't installed, Ansible installs it.

Flow:

First run:
Apache not installed
       ↓
Install Apache
       ↓
changed

Second run:
Apache already installed
       ↓
Nothing to change
       ↓
ok

c) update_cache: yes
update_cache: yes

This tells the apt module to update the package information before installing Apache.

It's similar to:

sudo apt update

So your task is effectively handling the equivalent of:

sudo apt update
sudo apt install apache2

through the Ansible module.

6. Your second task is:

- name: Copy index.html
  ansible.builtin.copy:
    src: index.html
    dest: /var/www/html/index.html
    owner: root
    group: root
    mode: '0644'

This task uses the copy module.

Its job is:

Copy a file from the Ansible control node to the managed server.

=> In your lab:

WSL
 │
 │ index.html
 │
 │ Ansible copy module
 ↓
EC2
 │
 └── /var/www/html/index.html

a) src
src: index.html

src means:

Where is the source file?

In your case:

index.html

is the HTML file in your Ansible project directory.

b) dest
dest: /var/www/html/index.html

dest means:

Where should the file be placed on the managed server?

Apache's default web directory on Ubuntu is:

/var/www/html/

So Ansible copies:

WSL:
index.html
     ↓
EC2:
/var/www/html/index.html

c) owner
owner: root

This sets the owner of the file to:

root

d) group
group: root

This sets the group ownership to:

root

So:

Owner → root
Group → root

d) mode: '0644'
mode: '0644'

This sets the Linux permissions of the HTML file.

0644 means:

Owner  → read + write
Group  → read
Others → read

In symbolic form:

rw-r--r--

7. How the Whole Playbook Works

When you run:

ansible-playbook -i inventory.ini first-playbook.yaml --limit app_key

Here, --limit means "run the playbook only on the specific hosts/groups I mention."

Ansible roughly follows this process:

Read inventory
      ↓
Read playbook
      ↓
Find hosts
      ↓
Connect to EC2 using SSH
      ↓
Use elevated privileges
      ↓
Run Task 1
      ↓
Install Apache
      ↓
Run Task 2
      ↓
Copy index.html
      ↓
Set ownership and permissions
      ↓
Finish

8. Playbook vs Ansible Command

a) You may have used:

ansible app_key -i inventory.ini -m ping

That's an ad-hoc command.

It is useful for performing a quick individual operation.

b) A playbook is better when you have multiple tasks.

=> Eg:

a) Ad-hoc:
Check connectivity → ping

b) Playbook:
Install Apache
Copy HTML
Set permissions
